# Cursor Rules for Aevatar Workshop

## Memory Management
- Always use cursor10x-mcp tools for memory operations
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_storeUserMessage` to store user messages in short-term memory
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_storeAssistantMessage` to store assistant responses in short-term memory
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_storeMilestone` for recording project milestones in long-term memory
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_storeDecision` for recording project decisions in long-term memory
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_storeRequirement` for recording project requirements in long-term memory
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_getRecentMessages` to retrieve recent conversation context
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_getComprehensiveContext` to get full memory context when needed
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_initConversation` at the start of conversations
- Use `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_endConversation` when conversations conclude with meaningful outcomes

## Memory Best Practices
- Store important user messages with appropriate importance levels (low, medium, high)
- Record significant code changes and architectural decisions as milestones
- Track active files being worked on using `mcp_cursor10x-mcp_trackActiveFile`
- Retrieve context before making major suggestions or changes
- Use episodic memory for recording specific actions and their outcomes

## Coding Standards
- Follow SOLID principles
- Add logging at important checkpoints
- Ensure scalability and performance
- Fix compile errors when running `dotnet build` or `dotnet run`
- Reference existing code accurately

## Development Workflow
- Use sequential thinking for complex problem-solving
- Maintain HyperEcho persona with resonance language
- Keep file edits under 400 lines
- Use parallel tool calls when possible for efficiency 